From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 18:14:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: dts: sophgo: reserve a ramoops region on the Milk-V
 Duo S

The Duo S is a headless board, and until now the only way to learn why it had
rebooted was to have been watching the serial console at the moment it did.
Nothing survived the restart: the kernel log lives in RAM, and a reboot starts
over with an empty one.

That got worse rather than better with PANIC_TIMEOUT=10 in the Armbian configs.
A panicking board used to sit there with the trace on the console, which at
least kept the evidence in front of anyone who came to look; now it restarts
itself after ten seconds, which is right for a board in a corner but takes the
trace with it.

So hold a megabyte back for pstore's ramoops backend. DRAM contents are not
cleared by a warm reset, so the region still holds the dying log when the
kernel comes back up, and pstore reads it out into /sys/fs/pstore, where it
stays until deleted.

The address is the interesting part, because both ends of memory are already
spoken for. U-Boot loads the kernel, dtb and initrd low - between 0x80200000
and 0x84000000 - and relocates the dtb and initrd high, just under the top of
DRAM, before handing over. The top is also where the c906l coprocessor's
region sits at 0x9fe00000 when that overlay is enabled, an address fixed by
CVIMMAP_FREERTOS_ADDR in the vendor memory map rather than by us. 0x90000000 is
in the middle, which nothing else claims, and is far enough below 0x9fe00000
that enabling the c906l overlay cannot collide with it - verified by building
the dtb and applying the overlay to it, which leaves both regions present and
the remoteproc node's memory-region unchanged.

no-map is deliberately absent, as it is on most in-tree ramoops nodes. The
region only has to be kept away from the page allocator; leaving it in the
kernel's memory map means pfn_valid() holds, and persistent_ram_buffer_map()
takes the vmap path rather than ioremap, which is the one meant for ordinary
DRAM.

Sizes are modest for a 512MB board: 32KB per dmesg record and 128KB of console
log, inside a 1MB region. ftrace and pmsg are left out, matching the pstore
options the configs enable.
---
 .../boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts    | 29 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
index faf924e..e7a31f4 100644
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
@@ -13,6 +13,35 @@
 		reg = <0x80000000 0x20000000>;
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * A megabyte held back for pstore's ramoops backend, so the tail of the
+	 * kernel log outlives a reboot. PANIC_TIMEOUT is 10 in the Armbian
+	 * configs, so a panicking board restarts itself and would otherwise take
+	 * the trace with it; DRAM survives a warm reset, and pstore finds the
+	 * region again on the next boot and presents it as /sys/fs/pstore.
+	 *
+	 * The address stays clear of both ends of DRAM, which are already spoken
+	 * for. U-Boot loads the kernel, dtb and initrd low, between 0x80200000
+	 * and 0x84000000, and relocates the dtb and initrd high, just under the
+	 * top of memory; the c906l overlay puts the coprocessor's region at
+	 * 0x9fe00000, where the vendor FSBL expects it. The middle is what
+	 * nothing else claims, and 0x90000000 is far enough below 0x9fe00000
+	 * that enabling that overlay cannot collide with this.
+	 *
+	 * No no-map, as on most in-tree ramoops nodes: the region only has to be
+	 * kept away from the page allocator. Left in the kernel's memory map,
+	 * pfn_valid() holds and persistent_ram_buffer_map() vmaps it rather than
+	 * falling back to ioremap, which is the path meant for ordinary DRAM.
+	 */
+	reserved-memory {
+		ramoops@90000000 {
+			compatible = "ramoops";
+			reg = <0x90000000 0x100000>;
+			record-size = <0x8000>;
+			console-size = <0x20000>;
+		};
+	};
+
 	aliases {
 		pwm0 = &pwm0;
 		pwm1 = &pwm1;
-- 
